# Project Stack & Architecture
- Frontend: React + TypeScript
- Backend: Node.js + TypeScript running on AWS Lambda + API Gateway (Serverless)
- Database: Live AWS DynamoDB (use @aws-sdk/client-dynamodb and @aws-sdk/lib-dynamodb v3)

# Serverless & DynamoDB Best Practices
- Initialize the DynamoDBDocumentClient outside the Lambda handler so connections are reused across warm invocations.
- Use strict TypeScript interfaces for Lambda events (APIGatewayProxyEventV2 / APIGatewayProxyEvent) and DynamoDB item schemas.
- Always configure DynamoDB tables with BillingMode: PAY_PER_REQUEST (On-Demand) so idle student projects incur near-zero AWS cost.
- Prefer Query over Scan to avoid accidental Read Capacity Unit (RCU) spikes on live tables.
- Follow least-privilege IAM permissions in infrastructure configs (grant only needed actions like dynamodb:GetItem, PutItem, Query, UpdateItem, DeleteItem on the specific table ARN).
- Never hardcode AWS access keys, table names, or ARNs—pass table names via Lambda environment variables (`process.env.TABLE_NAME`).

# Agent Behavior & Quota Efficiency
- Never read or index `node_modules/`, `.serverless/`, `.aws-sam/`, `cdk.out/`, `dist/`, or `.git/`.
- Before changing infrastructure config or multiple files, list a brief 3-bullet plan first.
- Keep edits scoped strictly to the requested feature or Lambda function.
